<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Volcae Tectosages</h1><p class="st-meta">300 BCE – 106 BCE (approx.) · capital Tolosa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Celtic people of Toulouse, kin of the Galatians of Anatolia, whose sacred gold was seized by a Roman consul in 106 BCE.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volcae">Volcae</a> were a Celtic people who settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/languedoc">Languedoc</a> in the 3rd century BCE. The Tectosages held the western part around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Tolosa</a> (<a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/county-of-toulouse">Toulouse</a>) on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garonne">Garonne</a>. Other bands of the same name joined the migrations into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, where the Galatian Tectosages settled around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankara">Ancyra</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Tolosa</a> controlled the corridor between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and the Atlantic. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> describes a rich sanctuary where gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> were sunk in sacred lakes. They became allies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> after the conquest of the Province in 121 BCE.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 107 BCE they rebelled with the help of the migrating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimbri">Cimbri</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigurini">Tigurini</a>. The consul <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-servilius-caepio-consul-106-bce">Quintus Servilius Caepio</a> retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Tolosa</a> in 106 BCE and seized its treasure, the gold of Tolosa, which vanished on the way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. Their land became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallia-narbonensis">Gallia Narbonensis</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/volcae-tectosages.jpg" alt="Volcae Tectosages" width="320" height="233"><figcaption>Image: Carole Raddato from FRANKFURT, Germany · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cinq_torques_gaulois,_IIIe_avant_J.-C.,_MSR,_Mus%C3%A9e_Saint-Raymond_(7221235672).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>300 BCE – 106 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tolosa</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Celtic polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Gaulish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Tectosages</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-203&amp;focus=volcae-tectosages" aria-label="Volcae Tectosages on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4715 632 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Volcae Tectosages lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5035 888L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5035 888h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5035 888h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=-203&amp;focus=volcae-tectosages">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

